WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:04.040
Julian Brothers Bakery is a community fixture
in this Detroit suburb.

00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:08.170
It’s one of the few small, locally run operations
left.

00:00:08.170 --> 00:00:12.550
Every morning for the past 48 years, Richard
Julian has put on his apron and served the

00:00:12.550 --> 00:00:16.150
neighborhood everything from deli meats to
birthday cakes.

00:00:16.150 --> 00:00:20.789
The bakery is also situated on the edge of
a county that was pivotal for Trump’s victory:

00:00:20.789 --> 00:00:23.070
Macomb County, outside of Detroit.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:27.960
Julian, like some of his employees and many
of his customers, voted for Trump.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:33.120
He thinks the President's term has been good
for his 30 employees, and his bakery.

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:41.410
"Donald Trump was a little much of a stretch
for me in the beginning, but when he became

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:46.500
the nominee of the party, I backed him, because
he was a businessman."

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:51.219
This is exactly the sort of small Middle American
establishment that was at the heart of trump’s

00:00:51.219 --> 00:00:56.690
campaign message: to lift up the little guy
and stick it to the "elites."

00:00:56.690 --> 00:01:01.440
Ann Celine Wydeven, an educator and neighborhood
resident, said she was attracted to Trump

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.670
and his message in part because of how different
he sounded.

00:01:04.670 --> 00:01:13.409
"The more I listened, and the more I heard
his concretizing ideas, those were my ideas.

00:01:13.409 --> 00:01:16.000
And, that's what I began to watch.

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.659
And I was, at first.

00:01:18.659 --> 00:01:24.460
In the first debate, I was almost inside the
TV; I thought 'What am I listening to?'

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.000
It was just very interesting.

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:27.680
It was so different.

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:29.300
And it wasn't staid.

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:31.259
It was just, different."

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:36.090
They don’t love everything about him: Everyone
we spoke with mentioned that his tweeting

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:40.509
can be cringe-worthy; that maybe he has a
tendency to speak without thinking.

00:01:40.509 --> 00:01:43.270
And sometimes his personal life can be messy.

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:47.810
But Joan Fernane, a bakery regular and lifelong
Michigan resident, said despite everything

00:01:47.810 --> 00:01:50.140
she wants to see the president succeed.

00:01:50.140 --> 00:01:57.360
"I'd like to see him take us back to the days
when people were people, not units or objects

00:01:57.360 --> 00:02:00.590
or numbers or classifications.

00:02:00.590 --> 00:02:06.439
Where people were people, and we have respect
for one another, and I like some of the things

00:02:06.439 --> 00:02:13.120
he's done on the spur of the moment, I just,
I really like the man, and I want to see him

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:17.340
continue with his goals and at least get most
of them accomplished."

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:21.620
Not everybody in the neighborhood is a Trump
supporter, and some family ties have been

00:02:21.620 --> 00:02:24.220
strained.

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.900
Gary Milburn, an employee at the bakery has
two daughters who do not like Trump.

00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:31.110
He says he understood their objections.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.260
But he still voted for Trump.

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:40.000
"My family is not thrilled with me because
you know I did vote for Trump.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:47.349
You know he's definitely many statements regarding
grabbing women by the whatever, and doing

00:02:47.349 --> 00:02:53.610
this and that, which to me is a lot of you
know locker room talk."

00:02:53.610 --> 00:02:57.570
Two years into Trump's first term as president,
the country is still divided.

00:02:57.570 --> 00:03:02.090
There have been shocking announcements, scandals,
treaties have been torn-up - but would these

00:03:02.090 --> 00:03:04.400
people vote for Trump again?

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:06.239
"Would you vote for him again?

00:03:06.239 --> 00:03:07.239
Yes I would."

00:03:07.239 --> 00:03:09.100
"If he runs again, I hope he wins."

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:13.459
"Oh I will vote for him again, I want this
to continue."

00:03:13.459 --> 00:03:17.730
"I will vote for him again, depending on who
he's going to be running against."

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:22.881
At this stage, a true republican challenger
looks unlikely during the 2020 campaign, and

00:03:22.881 --> 00:03:26.879
the Democrats will have a tall order to win
back the hearts of these voters.

00:03:26.879 --> 00:03:30.519
Here in Julian's Brothers Bakery it seems
Trump would have no trouble getting reelected

00:03:30.519 --> 00:03:33.000
for a second term.

